      "mergedPostDetails": "Hello! It is no secret that the Quests hand tracking is an incredibly powerful (albiet unutilized) feature of the mobile headset. I find this fair as hand controls in most VR games are still very impractical. In the case of social games however, I believe this can not only become possible, but an ideal way to interact. The following is my idea of how such a function might be implemented into VRChat.\n(Apologies in advance for the poor diagrams, was made extremely on the nose, however I think they push the point across fairly well.)\nEffectively, my idea is the option to tie a bracelet to human models. This would appear as a regular bracelet during normal activity, however, when the users wrist is flipped to show the inner arm, an icon will appear to \nproject on top of the bracelet. When the icon is tapped, a holographic screen is projected from the band, onto the users inner arm. This transparent surface would include a dragable circle, allowing the user to move in the direction that they choose to drag the circle, similar to the functionality of a mobile games on-screen joystick. \nAdditionally, this surface would allow for the usage of gestures, allowing for easy access to the menu. (For example two fingers swiping down on your arm to trigger said menu.)\n \nNow of course, such functionality is heavily dependent on the avatar used, as well if developers even choose to include the bracelet. I believe that a default gesture system would be the best way to go about working around this problem. This would consist of a similar holographic surface, however this may simply be triggered by tapping a floating icon, on doing a finger gesture. The surface would then be projected in front of the user, and would function identically to that of the bracelet.\nNow, this idea has some flaws, the primary being that some avatars may not be rigged with arm movement period. This I believe may be overlookable, as I have noticed with some broken avatars, grabbing and manipulating objects is still present, there is just no way to either see or move their hands. This may be something worth looking into in the future, but I do not believe it to be a massive deterrent. \nOverall, I believe that a bracelet based movement/menu system is the best way to go. As VRChat is by no means an intensive game, hand gestures would not deter from the overall experience. Additionally, having gestures done on the arm will allow the user to effectively use their body as the surface, allowing for deeper immersion. \nI have included diagrams of what such implementations look like. If you have any questions, my Discord handle is Zoara#4125. I can also be pinged from the VRChat Discord server. I hope my idea allows for a smooth implementation of hand tracking. Thank you for your time.",
      "mergedPostTitle": "Hand Tracking Implementation on Quest (and how it could be done)",
